 #### Rasterization-based Volume Modeling
-The process of building volume data voxel-by-voxel, considered the third layer in the voxel modeling abstraction hierarchy [S5, unofficial]. It solves the problem of assigning density values to voxels based on primitive contributions. It differs from instantiation-based modeling by pulling values into a voxel (similar to raytracing), whereas instantiation-based modeling pushes values into voxels (similar to micropolygon rendering) [S5, unofficial]. The main rasterization loop iterates over voxels in the discrete bounding box, transforms the voxel position to world space, samples the primitive's density function, and adds the value to the buffer if positive [S5, unofficial].
+The process of building volume data voxel-by-voxel, considered the third layer in the voxel modeling abstraction hierarchy [S5, unofficial]. It solves the problem of assigning density values to voxels based on primitive contributions. It differs from instantiation-based modeling by pulling values into a voxel (similar to raytracing), whereas instantiation-based modeling pushes values into voxels (similar to micropolygon rendering) [S5, unofficial]. The main rasterization loop iterates over voxels in the discrete bounding box, transforms voxel position to world space, samples the primitive, and adds the value to the buffer if positive [S5, unofficial].
 
 #### Single-Pass Voxelization
-A voxelization approach that utilizes the geometry shader to reduce draw calls by selecting the axis from which each triangle has the largest overall projection for fragment generation [S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of multiple draw calls in multi-pass approaches. It employs conservative rasterization to generate fragments as long as there is something inside the pixel, not only if its center is covered [S6, unofficial]. It uses an MSAA render target during the voxelization process to solve the issue of incorrectly filling empty cells caused by conservative rasterization generating too many fragments [S6, unofficial]. Hardware support for conservative rasterization is available on Nvidia Maxwell architecture and later; RTX driver support is available for Turing and Pascal architecture GPUs [S6, unofficial]. A pitfall is that GPUs only generate fragments if a pixel's center is covered by a triangle, leading to small cracks at the edges of triangles if conservative rasterization is not used [S6, unofficial].
+A voxelization approach that utilizes the geometry shader to reduce draw calls by selecting the axis from which each triangle has the largest overall projection for fragment generation [S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of multiple draw calls for objects with the same material. It employs conservative rasterization to generate fragments as long as there is something inside the pixel, not only if its center is covered [S6, unofficial]. It uses an MSAA render target to solve the issue of incorrectly filling empty cells caused by conservative rasterization generating too many fragments [S6, unofficial]. Hardware support for conservative rasterization is available on Nvidia Maxwell architecture and later; RTX driver support is available for Turing and Pascal architecture GPUs [S6, unofficial]. A pitfall is that GPUs only generate fragments if a pixel's center is covered by a triangle, leading to small cracks at the edges of triangles [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{faces} \times N_{voxels\_in\_bbox})$, where $N_{faces}$ is the number of triangles and $N_{voxels\_in\_bbox}$ is the number of voxels in the bounding box of each triangle [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{occupied\_voxels})$ when using a hash table [S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** GPU-based variants offer significant speedups (e.g., 50x) for high-resolution grids compared to CPU-based methods [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient for sparse meshes; leverages existing rasterization hardware; simple to implement [S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Output mesh size can be very large compared to input; standard rasterization may produce holes without conservative techniques [S2, S6, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a surface representation (voxel mesh) is needed for voxel art or simplified collision detection, and the mesh is sparse [S2, S4, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When deep voxelization (interior voxels) is required, as this method typically performs shell voxelization [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of triangles $N_t$ and the number of voxels $N_v$ in the bounding box. For sparse hash-based methods, complexity is roughly $O(N_t \cdot B + N_v)$, where $B$ is the average number of voxels per triangle bounding box [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_v)$ for sparse hash tables, significantly less than $O(R_x R_y R_z)$ for dense grids [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** GPU-based methods offer significant speedups (e.g., 50x) for high-resolution grids compared to CPU implementations [S1]. CPU methods are simpler to implement but slower for large meshes [S1][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient for sparse data; leverages existing graphics hardware; produces clean surface meshes [S1][S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Output mesh size can be very large (e.g., 144,968 vertices for a simple torus with voxel size 0.5) [S2, unofficial]; prone to holes without conservative rasterization or precision tuning [S2, S6, unofficial]; requires careful handling of precision to avoid artifacts [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When a surface mesh representation of a voxelized object is needed, or when memory efficiency is critical for sparse scenes [S1][S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When interior density information is required (deep voxelization) [S2, unofficial]; when exact topological correctness is critical without multi-view passes [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the need to convert polygonal models into voxel formats for real-time rendering and voxel art applications. It is implemented in libraries such as Point-Cloud-Utils [S1] and the header-only C library by Karim Naaji [S4, unofficial]. It is also used in Python via `madcad` [S3, unofficial]. The technique is distinct from ray-tracing voxelization and is often compared to geometry shader-based approaches, with rasterization-based methods generally providing better surface accuracy [S2, unofficial].
+Rasterization-based voxelization originated from the need to efficiently convert polygonal models into volumetric data for rendering and simulation. The Point Cloud Utils library popularized a Python/C++ interface for this task [S1]. The C library `voxelizer` by Karim Naaji provided a header-only solution for shell voxelization, demonstrating the trade-offs in mesh size [S2, unofficial]. The `madcad` library introduced the `PositionMap` hasher for efficient sparse voxelization in Python [S3, unofficial]. Theoretical foundations are discussed in production volume rendering literature, which categorizes rasterization as a "pull" method for volume modeling [S5, unofficial]. Real-time applications have explored GPU acceleration via compute shaders and geometry shaders to overcome CPU bottlenecks [S1][S6, unofficial].
 
